WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.252
A clean Data frame is useful, but a reproducible cleaning process is much more valuable.

00:00:06.252 --> 00:00:13.532
You should be able to explain what issue you found, what action you took, how many values were affected,

00:00:13.532 --> 00:00:16.097
and why the decision was reasonable.

00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:19.575
The example on the slide shows a simple cleaning log.

00:00:20.126 --> 00:00:24.513
Imagine that another analyst receives your dataset six months later.

00:00:24.513 --> 00:00:32.323
They should not have to guess why Web and web became the same category or why certain rows disappeared.

00:00:32.323 --> 00:00:39.737
In real projects, keep cleaning steps in code rather than manually editing the original file.

00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:47.372
A notebook, script, or pipeline creates an audit trail that can be rerun when new data arrives.

00:00:47.372 --> 00:00:51.788
Good documentation also protects you from your own memory.

00:00:51.788 --> 00:00:58.496
If you return to the project later, the code and notes explain exactly what was changed and why.
